<% content_for :title, "#{t('legal.terms.title')} · #{t('app_name')}" %>
<% content_for :description, t("legal.terms.meta_description") %>

<main id="main" class="spread py-12">
  <%= render "legal/head",
        eyebrow: t("legal.eyebrow.terms"),
        title: t("legal.terms.title"),
        updated_on: LegalController::LAST_UPDATED %>

  <article class="legal-doc">
    <p class="lede">
      These terms govern the use of aperto.wine, the service that lets a
      restaurant publish its wine list and lets whoever is at the table read it
      and send an order. They come in two parts: one for guests, one for
      restaurateurs.
    </p>

    <h2>What aperto.wine is</h2>
    <p>
      aperto.wine is a technical service. We do not sell wine, hold it, serve it
      or take payment for it. Every wine list is published by the restaurant
      that maintains it, on its own responsibility; we provide the tool it uses
      to publish that list and receive orders.
    </p>

    <h2>For guests</h2>
    <h3>The contract is with the restaurant</h3>
    <p>
      Sending an order places a request <strong>with the restaurant</strong>: the
      arrangement about the wine is between the guest and the restaurant, and
      begins and ends there. aperto.wine is only the <strong>technical
      intermediary</strong> that carries the request, and is not a party to it.
    </p>
    <h3>An order is a request, not a confirmation</h3>
    <p>
      The restaurant may accept an order, adjust it with the table, or decline it
      — for instance when a bottle has just run out. Availability, vintages,
      formats and prices shown on the menu are stated by the restaurant and may
      be corrected in the dining room. Payment happens at the table by whatever
      means the venue accepts: no payment passes through aperto.wine.
    </p>
    <h3>Alcohol and minors</h3>
    <p>
      Serving alcoholic drinks to anyone under <strong>18</strong> is prohibited
      by Article 14-ter of Italian Law 125/2001. The check belongs to the
      restaurant's staff, who may ask for identification before serving and may
      refuse to serve. Reading the list or sending an order through this service
      is not a substitute for that check and confers no right to be served.
    </p>
    <h3>Allergens and product information</h3>
    <p>
      Information about wines, ingredients and allergens — sulphites included —
      is entered and kept up to date by the restaurant, which is responsible for
      it as the food business operator under Reg. (EU) 1169/2011 and Italian
      Legislative Decree 231/2017. If you have an allergy or intolerance,
      <strong>always ask the dining room staff</strong> before ordering:
      aperto.wine neither verifies nor warrants that data.
    </p>
    <h3>Proper use of the service</h3>
    <p>
      The menu and order sending are open and require no account. You must not
      use the service to send false or automated orders, to attempt to reach the
      data of other tables or other venues, or to interfere with how the service
      runs.
    </p>

    <h2>For restaurateurs</h2>
    <h3>Account and use of the service</h3>
    <p>
      The account belongs to the proprietor or to whoever they authorise;
      credentials must be kept safe and everything done with them is attributed
      to the account. We grant a non-exclusive, non-transferable, revocable right
      to use the service for running your own business.
    </p>
    <h3>Responsibility for content</h3>
    <p>
      The restaurateur is responsible for the accuracy of what they publish: wine
      and producer names, vintages, prices, formats, availability, allergen and
      sulphite information, venue details. They are equally responsible for
      complying with the rules governing their business — licences to serve,
      food information duties, the prohibition on serving minors, tax
      obligations.
    </p>
    <h3>Availability, suspension and withdrawal</h3>
    <p>
      We work to keep the service running but do not guarantee uninterrupted
      availability: maintenance, provider failures and force majeure can
      interrupt it. We may suspend an account used in breach of these terms or
      of the law, giving notice where possible. The restaurateur may stop using
      the service at any time; on closure, data is deleted or returned on their
      instructions.
    </p>

    <h2>Appointment as processor (Article 28 GDPR)</h2>
    <p>
      For guests' personal data — orders, tables, the name given — the
      restaurateur is the <strong>controller</strong> and aperto.wine is the
      <strong>processor</strong>. By accepting these terms the parties enter into
      the agreement required by Article 28 GDPR, on the following conditions.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Subject matter, duration, nature and purpose.</strong> The
        processing consists of collecting, storing and making available order
        and table data, for the sole purpose of providing the service, for the
        duration of the relationship.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Categories of data and data subjects.</strong> Minimal
        identifying data (the name a guest gives), order and table data,
        technical connection data; data subjects: the venue's guests and the
        staff the controller grants access to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Documented instructions.</strong> We process the data only on the
        controller's instructions and never for our own purposes. If we believe
        an instruction infringes data protection law, we say so.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Confidentiality.</strong> Anyone on our side with access to the
        data is authorised and bound by confidentiality.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Security (Article 32).</strong> We maintain appropriate technical
        and organisational measures: encryption in transit, credentials stored as
        hashes, per-venue separation of data, least-privilege access, and backups
        managed by the hosting provider.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sub-processors.</strong> The controller gives general
        authorisation for the sub-processors listed in the
        <%= link_to "privacy notice", privacy_path %>. Any addition or
        replacement is announced with reasonable notice; the controller may
        object and, in that case, terminate the service.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Assistance.</strong> We assist the controller in answering data
        subject requests (Arts. 15–22) and in meeting the obligations in
        Arts. 32–36, including breach notification: we report breaches to the
        controller without undue delay after becoming aware of them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Deletion or return.</strong> When the relationship ends we delete
        the data or return it, as the controller instructs, save where retention
        is required by law.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Audits.</strong> We make available the information needed to
        demonstrate compliance with these obligations and allow audits, at times
        and in a manner agreed between the parties.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Transfers.</strong> Processing takes place in the European Union;
        any transfer outside the EU will occur only under the safeguards in
        Chapter V GDPR and after informing the controller.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Intellectual property</h2>
    <p>
      The software, name and design of aperto.wine remain the operator's. Content
      uploaded by the restaurateur remains theirs: they authorise us to host it
      and show it on the public menu for as long as providing the service
      requires. Address data comes from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap
      contributors, ODbL licence).
    </p>

    <h2>Limitation of liability</h2>
    <p>
      We are not answerable for what the restaurant publishes or serves, nor for
      the quality, information or availability of the products. To the extent the
      law allows, our liability towards the restaurateur is limited to direct and
      foreseeable loss. Nothing in these terms limits the mandatory rights of
      consumers.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
    <p>
      We may update these terms; the date at the top of the page marks the
      version in force. Changes material to restaurateurs are announced in
      advance, and continuing to use the service after an update counts as
      acceptance.
    </p>

    <h2>Governing law and jurisdiction</h2>
    <p>
      Italian law applies. For relationships with restaurateurs the courts of the
      place where the operator has its registered office have jurisdiction. For
      guests acting as consumers, the mandatory jurisdiction of the courts where
      they reside or are domiciled remains unaffected, under the Italian
      Consumer Code.
    </p>

    <%= render "legal/operator_identity" %>
  </article>

  <%= render "legal/foot",
        other_document_path: privacy_path,
        other_document_label: t("legal.privacy.title") %>
</main>
